new file mode 100644
index 0000000..1b98d7d
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/deployment.md
@@ -0,0 +1,136 @@
+# Deployment
+
+Fizzy is set up to run on a single Linux server with [Kamal](https://kamal-deploy.org/).
+Everything runs in one container:
+
+- Puma serving the Rails app (through [Thruster](https://github.com/basecamp/thruster))
+- Solid Queue processing background jobs inside the web process
+- SQLite databases and local Active Storage uploads in `/rails/storage`
+
+[kamal-proxy](https://github.com/basecamp/kamal-proxy) runs on the same box,
+terminates TLS, and obtains a Let's Encrypt certificate for your hostname, so
+Fizzy is served over HTTPS at `https://your-hostname`.
+
+## Prerequisites
+
+- A Linux server (amd64 or arm64) with Docker installed. Ports 80 and 443 must
+  be reachable from the internet; `bin/kamal setup` can install Docker over SSH
+  if it is missing.
+- A DNS `A` and/or `AAAA` record for the hostname you want to use, pointing at
+  the server.
+- SSH access to the server. Kamal uses `root` by default; if you deploy as
+  another user with `sudo`, uncomment and set `ssh.user` in `config/deploy.yml`.
+- Docker on your own machine, plus Ruby with this repository's bundle installed.
+- An SMTP server that can send mail from your Fizzy hostname. Fizzy uses
+  passwordless email sign-in, so it needs to deliver verification codes.
+
+## 1. Configure Fizzy
+
+Edit `config/deploy.yml`:
+
+| Setting | What to change |
+| --- | --- |
+| `servers.web` | Your server's IP address or hostname (the SSH target). |
+| `proxy.host` | The public hostname Fizzy will be served from. |
+| `APP_HOST` | The same public hostname, so generated links and emails use it. |
+| `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS` and the `SMTP_*` settings | Your mail server's details. |
+| `builder.arch` | Use `arm64` if your server is ARM64; otherwise keep `amd64`. |
+
+`SMTP_AUTHENTICATION` is usually `plain` or `login`; check your mail provider's
+documentation. If your mail server does not require authentication, remove
+`SMTP_USERNAME` and `SMTP_AUTHENTICATION` and do not set `SMTP_PASSWORD`.
+
+## 2. Configure your secrets
+
+Copy the example environment file and fill it in:
+
+```sh
+cp .env.example .env
+```
+
+Generate a secret key base (it signs cookies and sessions):
+
+```sh
+bin/rails secret
+```
+
+Put the generated value and your SMTP password in `.env`:
+
+```sh
+SECRET_KEY_BASE=<the generated value>
+SMTP_PASSWORD=<your SMTP password>
+```
+
+`bin/kamal` loads `.env` automatically, and the file is gitignored. You can
+also export these variables in your shell instead of using `.env`. If a value
+contains spaces or shell metacharacters, wrap it in quotes (for example,
+`SMTP_PASSWORD="p@ss word"`).
+
+If you use Rails encrypted credentials, run `bin/rails credentials:edit` once;
+this creates `config/master.key`, which `.kamal/secrets` reads automatically.
+It is optional here because the deployment uses `SECRET_KEY_BASE` from the
+environment.
+
+## 3. Deploy
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal setup
+```
+
+On the first run, Kamal starts a local Docker registry tunnel, builds the
+image, pushes it, boots the container, runs `db:prepare` to create and migrate
+the SQLite databases, and asks kamal-proxy for a TLS certificate. Open
+`https://your-hostname` when it finishes.
+
+To log in, create the first account on that page; Fizzy emails a six-character
+verification code to the address you provide.
+
+## Deploying updates
+
+Run the same commands after pulling new code:
+
+```sh
+git pull
+bin/kamal deploy
+```
+
+The `fizzy_storage` volume is mounted at `/rails/storage`, which holds all four
+SQLite databases (`production.sqlite3`, plus the cable, cache, and queue
+databases) and uploaded files. It is reused between deploys, so your data
+survives image and container changes. Avoid `bin/kamal remove` unless you truly
+want to remove the app containers (the named volume is preserved by default).
+
+## Background jobs
+
+Solid Queue runs inside the Puma container because `SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA` is set
+to `true` in `config/deploy.yml`. It processes the queues configured in
+`config/queue.yml` and the recurring tasks in `config/recurring.yml`. There is
+no separate worker container or service to manage.
+
+## Backups
+
+The simplest safe backup is to stop the app container, archive the volume on
+the server, and start it again:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal app stop
+ssh root@your-server 'docker run --rm -v fizzy_storage:/data \
+  -v "$HOME":/backup alpine tar czf /backup/fizzy-$(date +%F).tar.gz -C /data .'
+bin/kamal app start
+scp root@your-server:'~/fizzy-*.tar.gz' .
+```
+
+Adjust the SSH user and path to match your server. For smaller exports, the
+app's own per-account export feature writes ZIP files to
+`/rails/storage/production/files`.
+
+## Troubleshooting
+
+- Confirm DNS points at the server and that ports 80 and 443 are open. The TLS
+  certificate cannot be issued otherwise.
+- Follow logs with `bin/kamal app logs -f` (add `-r web` if needed).
+- Open a shell in the container with
+  `bin/kamal app exec --interactive --reuse "/bin/bash"`.
+- If SSH as `root` is not allowed, set `ssh.user` in `config/deploy.yml`.
+- If the server uses a different CPU architecture than your machine, update
+  `builder.arch` in `config/deploy.yml`.
